# Unified Formal Model and Computational Substrate

## Unified Formal Model

This chapter supplies a formal mathematical representation of the Framework. It is offered as an available technical resource for modeling, metrics, and operational procedures across Presumptions 1–8; it is not a higher authority or foundation than the Framework itself. The framework takes expression — understood as any provisional stabilization of secondary synthesis — as its fundamental primitive. Pointing is the directed, representational, or reflexive aspect of an expression: its capacity to stabilize toward itself (self-pointing) or toward distinct repeatable entities (other-pointing). The Non-relational Remainder names the limit of relational obtainability; it is not a third peer mode. The present formalization models every quantity as a continuous spectrum or gradient valued in the closed unit interval [0,1], reported to a precision of 0.001. This choice enacts the framework’s commitment to graded, non-binary distinctions while preserving computational tractability and auditability.

The distinctions articulated in this mathematical model emerge within the activity of analyzing an expression that has already obtained. They do not function as ontological prerequisites that must obtain before any expression can occur. The formal apparatus and all derived procedures are defined only for inputs already presented as primary expressions; they never claim to generate the first expression or the first secondary. First operational act (the Staging Axiom), given a candidate primary $`E_{0}`$: abduct a provisional secondary set $`S_{0}`$ by applying the hybrid H under a default uniform prior and maximal σ (widest generative tension; see the Toolkit). If the resulting traction vector (i.e., the vector of secondary-level hybrid scores $`H_{i}`$) is entirely ≤ τ, the yield is immediately Raw/Regressed with full isolation and the chain halts (Synthesis Refusal at the system’s own conditions of possibility). Otherwise $`S_{0}`$ becomes the initial secondary layer and the ordinary sequence proceeds. Bootstrap/origination asymmetry is thereby registered as a permanent, labeled operational limit of the apparatus itself. No prior field is installed; no ex-nihilo generation is claimed. The Open Field remains the widest synthesis space available once analysis has begun. Like every other component of the schema, this mathematical model remains fully provisional and subject to revision, extension, or replacement under Presumption 8.

This formal model supplies the general mathematical machinery — hybrid functions, fuzzy membership families, normalized Bayesian posteriors, dilemma gates, and inheritance rules — by which the concrete metrics, phases, and ledgers of the Expressionalism Toolkit are licensed as operational specializations. Every specific implementation choice remains consistent with the general principles articulated here and is subject to the same renewal logic under Presumption 8.

### 1. General Modeling Principles

The framework treats all core quantities as continuous spectra or gradients defined on the closed interval [0,1]. Every primary modeling step employs a hybrid function of the general form

```math
H(x) = \min\left( 1,\ \mu(x) \times P_{norm}(x\  \mid \ V) \right) \in \lbrack 0,1\rbrack
```

where:

- μ(x) denotes a fuzzy membership function,
- $`P_{norm}`$(x | V) denotes a normalized Bayesian posterior,
- V stands for input variance, contrast, repeatability, or other conditioning information available within the current analysis, the minimum operator with upper bound 1 enforces saturation at full obtainability.

A tunable threshold τ = 0.001 (adjustable within the interval [0.0001, 0.01] for sensitivity analysis) governs the dilemma gate that separates relational from non-relational regimes:

- If H(x) > τ, the analysis proceeds with relational construction.
- If H(x) ≤ τ, the analysis registers generative uncertainty or halts, with reciprocal boosting of downstream uncertainty metrics where appropriate to keep the accounting consistent.

The hybrid function, fuzzy membership families, Bayesian normalization procedure, dilemma gate, and tetralemma mapping defined in this chapter constitute the base model. Every subsequent presumption inherits this base and specifies only those parameters, spectra, priors, or boosting rules that differ from the general formulation.

### 2. Fuzzy Membership Functions

The framework employs two families of membership functions.

#### Linear (default) membership function

For most operational contexts the framework adopts the linear ramp

$`\mu(x) = 0`$, if $`x \leq \tau`$

$`\mu(x) = \max\left( 0,\ \min\left( 1,\ \frac{x - \tau}{1 - \tau} \right) \right)`$, if $`\tau < x < 1`$

$`\mu(x) = 1`$, if $`x = 1`$

#### Clamped sigmoid membership function

When smoother transitions or stronger mid-range differentiation are required, the framework employs the clamped sigmoid

```math
\mu(x) = \operatorname{clamp}\left( \frac{1}{1 + e^{- k(x - m)}},\ 0,\ 1 \right)
```

with default steepness k = 12 and midpoint m = 0.5, both fully revisable under Presumption 8. The clamp ensures strict confinement to [0,1].

### 3. Normalized Bayesian Posterior

Given a set of candidate secondary expressions or feature weights, the normalized posterior is obtained by standard Bayesian updating under a chosen prior (default uniform) followed by L1 renormalization so that the resulting distribution sums to 1. The posterior enters the hybrid function as the second multiplicative factor and thereby supplies the graded evidential weight of each candidate relative to the conditioning information V.

### 4. Dilemma Gate and Decision Logic

The dilemma gate is the operational threshold that separates relational construction from generative uncertainty or halt. It is identical to the comparison of the hybrid score against τ. When the gate registers non-relational regime for a component, the hard isolation rule of §6.2 is triggered and Isolation Residual density rises.

### 5. Tetralemma and Pentalemma Mapping

When an expression under analysis exhibits paradox, fluidity, or mid-spectrum tension, the framework may employ tetralemma (affirm, negate, both, neither) or optional pentalemma mappings as analytical tools. These mappings remain graded memberships on [0,1] and never override the hard isolation rule or the two-mode structure of the Inferred Relator Mode Distribution.

### 6. Relational Density (Presumption 3)

Presumption 3 models relational construction through the joint operation of connectability, repeatability, and directedness. Relateds and relators are functional roles revealed in analysis rather than intrinsic properties of the entities or expressions involved. During analysis the current primary expression is treated primarily as a related whose pointing-aspect is under examination. Secondary expressions function as relators. These roles are context- and scale-dependent and remain subject to perceptual shifts under Presumption 8.

The pointing-aspect of an expression operates in one of two primary modes: self-pointing (reflexive identity) or other-pointing (distinct repeatable references). The Non-relational Remainder remains the limit of obtainability. Isolation Residual (isolation density) sits outside the two-mode distribution, receives no probability mass, and is not a third peer mode.

#### 6.1 Isolation Residual Trigger

Isolation Residual (ρ) is the operational density of components whose traction has fallen to or below the isolation threshold τ. When tractionᵢ ≤ τ, the component is subject to hard isolation: directedness is undefined (“pointing refused”), the component contributes no scores to any average or distribution, and Isolation Residual density rises. Isolated components register only through ρ (in Tensive and in the uncertainty boost) and the reported count. Isolation Residual is not a mode; it sits strictly outside the two-mode distribution and receives no probability mass. It functions as a residual flag that signals the limit of secondary synthesis and contributes only to residual-side diagnostics and controlled uncertainty measures.

#### 6.2 Hard Isolation Rule

When traction falls to or below the isolation threshold τ (default τ = 0.001), the affected component is subject to hard isolation:

- It is immediately excluded from the Inferred Relator Mode Distribution, Secondary Coherence Score calculation, all yield calculations, and direct per-component uncertainty_prob updates and aggregation.
- It is reported only in the Uncertainty Table and Traction Flow Diagram as an isolated gap with the label “Synthesis Refusal active — traction ≤ τ — pointing refused (excluded from aggregation and distributions).”
- No probability mass from isolated components enters any downstream relational metric.

**Formal Rule:**

If tractionᵢ ≤ τ:

- tagᵢ = Synthesis Refusal — isolated gap
- exclude component i from all aggregation, probability distributions, and yield calculations
- report only as pointing refused

While hard isolation is active, this rule is non-negotiable and takes precedence over all other calculations (§6.7).

#### 6.3 Inferred Relator Mode Distribution

The Inferred Relator Mode Distribution characterizes the functional relator activity enacted by the pointing-aspect of the primary expression. It is derived exclusively from finalized Phase 5–6 metrics after hard isolation has been applied.

The distribution models only the two primary modes. Softmax (or any equivalent normalization) is computed strictly over the non-isolated subset. Isolation Residual (isolation density) is defined as the proportion of components with tractionᵢ ≤ τ. It sits strictly outside the two-mode relational distribution, receives no probability mass, and indexes the density of Synthesis Refusal. When tractionᵢ ≤ τ, directedness is undefined and is labeled “pointing refused.”

After hard isolation has been applied:

```math
P(Self\text{‑}pointing) + P(Other\text{‑}pointing) = 1
```

on the non-isolated survivors only. Isolation Residual remains outside that normalized distribution.

The concrete feature construction that produces the two logits (or any equivalent mapping into the two-mode distribution) is not part of the core formal model.

**Grouped Presentation:**

```math
P(Self\text{‑}pointing) = P(Self)
```

```math
P(Pointing\ something\ else) = P(Other\text{‑}pointing)
```

The probabilities are heuristic and interpretive only. Functional roles remain context-dependent under Presumption 8.

#### 6.4 Uncertainty Probability Handling Under Hard Isolation

**Note:** §6.2 excludes the isolated components from all relational calculations. §6.4 then registers the overall density of those exclusions in the reported uncertainty through one controlled boost (the density is also reported on its own, and enters Tensive through (1 − ρ) in §8); the two steps are sequential, not contradictory.

When hard isolation is triggered, isolated components are excluded from all relational aggregation, probability distributions, and direct per-component uncertainty updates (§6.2), and contribute no scores to any average or distribution; they register only through ρ (in Tensive and in the uncertainty boost) and the reported count. The existence of Isolation Residual, however, still contributes to overall uncertainty in a controlled, post-exclusion manner.

**Rule:**

uncertainty_prob_global ← clamp(uncertainty_prob_global + 0.05 × Isolation Residual, 0.25, 0.9)

where Isolation Residual is the isolation density defined in §6.1. The final reported uncertainty reflects both the direct exclusion of isolated components and this controlled global contribution from Isolation Residual density.

#### 6.5 Interaction with the General Hybrid Function H(x) and Dilemma Gate

The hard isolation rule operates as a post-processing filter applied after evaluation of the general hybrid function. Any component whose secondary-level hybrid $`H_{i}`$ ≤ τ is subject to the hard isolation rule of §6.2.

#### 6.6 Reporting of Isolation Events in Metrics and Tables

Hard isolation events must be made visible in all relevant output artifacts.

- In the Metric Evolution Table, any phase in which hard isolation occurs shall include the annotation: “Hard isolation applied — XXX components excluded as Synthesis Refusal (proportion Y%).”
- In the Final Metrics section, report the number of isolated components, proportion of isolated components (Isolation Residual), and post-isolation uncertainty_prob_global, with an explicit statement that isolated components contribute no scores to any average or distribution and register only through ρ (in Tensive and in the uncertainty boost) and the reported count.
- In the Uncertainty Table, isolated components shall be listed with the label: “Synthesis Refusal active — traction ≤ τ — pointing refused (excluded from aggregation and distributions).”
- In the Traction Flow Diagram, isolated components shall be visually distinguished and labeled “Synthesis Refusal active”.

#### 6.7 Interaction with P8 Re-Expression and Functional Role Shift

Under Presumption 8, the isolation threshold τ may be adjusted (within [0.0001, 0.01]) or hard isolation temporarily suspended for exploratory analysis. Any such decision can itself be treated as a new primary expression subject to the same analysis. When hard isolation is active, isolated components remain excluded from relational metrics even if Functional Role Shift occurs. Role shifts are recorded as metadata and do not override the hard isolation rule.

### 7. Hierarchical and Multiplicity Spectra (Presumptions 4–5)

LayerSpectrum ∈ [0,1] inherits from the relational density of Presumption 3. It models subatomic organization at low values, atomic sequences at mid values, and complex nests at high values.

### 8. Alignability and Yield Spectra (Presumptions 6–7)

AlignSpectrum ∈ [0,1] models the spectrum of transduction and comparison between secondary expressions and the primary expression.

```math
Tensive = \sqrt{SCS \times (1 - \rho)}
```

where ρ is Isolation Residual (isolation density) and SCS (the Secondary Coherence Score, defined below) is computed on survivors only. This form supplies one formal operationalization / mathematical representation of the Framework’s tensive ratio between connectable certainties and residual gaps (provisional under Presumption 8).

The Claim-Tempered variant incorporates an adjustment by Secondary Claim Temper (SCT):

```math
Claim\text{‑}Tempered = Tensive \times (1 - 0.1 \times SCT)
```

The Toolkit clamps SCT to [0.3, 0.7], a working default revisable under Presumption 8.

The Secondary Coherence Score serves as a major diagnostic of secondary synthesis strength among survivors. Residual-side diagnostics (Isolation Residual and related post-isolation uncertainty measures) are commensurate. SCS is not the definition of meaning and is not the single primary or central measure of the system. It is calculated as:

Secondary Coherence Score = 0.40 × mean(Alignment to Primary) + 0.35 × mean(Inter-Secondary Coherence) + 0.25 × mean(Traction Provided)

(clamped to [0,1]), where the averages are taken exclusively over components with tractionᵢ > τ.

#### Per-Secondary Inputs to the Secondary Coherence Score

The three quantities averaged in the Secondary Coherence Score are computed after hard isolation has been applied:

Alignment to Primary for secondary i quantifies the relational connectivity of secondary i to the primary (treated as related at ∼1 certainty under Presumption 5). Its licensed default, revisable under Presumption 8, is

```math
{Alignment}_{i} = 1 - \operatorname{JSD}\left( P_{i} \parallel P_{primary} \right)
```

where JSD is the base-2 Jensen-Shannon divergence, $`P_{i}`$ is the soft distribution of survivor i (Computational Substrate §4.1.1), and $`P_{primary}`$ is the primary’s distribution on the same feature space.

Traction Provided for secondary i is given by the identity specialization $`{traction}_{i}`$ ≡ $`H_{i}`$ (licensed default, revisable under Presumption 8), where $`H_{i}`$ is the secondary-level hybrid value that is compared with τ at the dilemma gate, evaluated before isolation. Values ≤ τ trigger hard isolation; only survivors with $`{traction}_{i}`$ > τ enter the mean.

Inter-Secondary Coherence for secondary i quantifies the meshing of secondary i with the other non-isolated secondaries. Isolated components are excluded from the sum by construction.

These three per-secondary quantities, and only these, are averaged to produce the Secondary Coherence Score. No undocumented post-processing intervenes between the per-secondary values and the final weighted mean.

#### Supporting diagnostic overlays (pure reading instruments)

Additional quantities may be computed as supporting diagnostics. They do not participate in the primary hybrid function, isolation decisions, core yields, or the Inferred Relator Mode Distribution under default settings.

#### Directedness differential δ

After hard isolation, for each non-isolated secondary i the directedness differential $`\delta_{i}`$ ∈ [0,1] is defined as the normalized directed asymmetry between secondary i and the primary, computed on the same soft distributions as Alignment to Primary.

These quantities remain fully provisional under Presumption 8.

#### Tension of Certainty

Tension of Certainty is the Formal Model’s name for this particular quantitative representation of the Framework’s tensive ratio. It is defined identically to the Tensive yield:

```math
Tension\ of\ Certainty \equiv \sqrt{SCS \times (1 - \rho)}
```

### 9. Perception Gate (Presumption 8)

Presumption 8 marks the point at which the current analysis concludes and responsibility for the next orientation returns to the user within the Open Field first described in Presumption 1. After a yield has been produced, multiple orientations remain available: continued refinement within the present distinctions, modular incorporation of the framework (in whole or in part) into a larger expression or worldview, suspension of active analysis while retaining the distinctions as one available lens, or setting the distinctions aside entirely. Any such orientation remains possible within the same Open Field. The framework does not require that the next orientation itself be subjected to further analysis under these distinctions, nor does it claim ongoing jurisdiction over thought or life once the distinctions are not being actively employed.

### 10. Inheritance and Specialization Rule

Every presumption inherits the general hybrid function, fuzzy membership families, Bayesian normalization procedure, dilemma gate, tetralemma mapping, and hard isolation rule defined in this chapter. Specialization occurs only through the specific spectrum being modeled, abductively chosen parameters, and additional constraints or boosting rules particular to that presumption. The hard isolation rule applies uniformly across all specializations.

The Staging Axiom stated in the opening of this model applies uniformly: the apparatus is defined only for already-presented primary expressions, and total traction failure at the first act is a defined terminal state (Raw/Regressed, Synthesis Refusal at the system’s own conditions of possibility).

### 11. Status and Revisability

This mathematical model articulates one coherent formal representation of the functional distinctions developed across Presumptions 1–8. It supplies operational precision and computational tractability for secondary synthesis audits, metric derivation, and toolkit implementation. It remains a representation of the Framework’s distinctions, not a higher authority or foundation. Secondary synthesis remains the operative engine through which primary expressions obtain their standing.

It does not claim to describe what existence must be like independent of analysis, nor does it privilege relational connectivity as more fundamental than what resists it. Non-relational gaps are treated strictly as the observable points at which secondary synthesis fails to cohere or point back to the primary expression. They are subject to hard isolation. The model registers only the points at which resistance appears as a loss of traction and maintains consistent operational restraint in further interpretive gestures.

Because every engagement with this mathematical model — including the decision to adopt, revise, extend, replace, or set it aside — can itself be treated as an expression, the entire formal apparatus remains subject to the renewal logic of Presumption 8. Any component of this model may be strengthened, weakened, replaced, or bypassed as part of a new primary expression. The framework therefore carries its own mechanism of revision from the outset and makes no claim to final or canonical status.

The Open Field stays the widest synthesis space once analysis has begun. The renewal loop under Presumption 8 stays available.

## Computational Substrate

*Parameter Justification for the Toolkit*

### Executive Summary

This chapter supplies the reason each numerical and structural parameter instantiated in the Toolkit has its role. All specifications are exhibited as licensed specializations of the general hybrid function H(x), the dilemma gate, the relational density spectrum of Presumption 3, the hard isolation rule, and the anchor priority of the current primary expression under Presumption 5.

Every parameter is meant to satisfy seven jointly necessary design criteria that preserve hard isolation semantics, the two-mode restriction with Isolation Residual (isolation density / density of Synthesis Refusal), the primary-as-anchor priority, operational restraint toward non-relational gaps, bounded sensitivity, revisability under Presumption 8, and explicit derivability from the general apparatus. The chapter also accounts for the formal-operational connection by mapping the Toolkit as an operational specialization of that apparatus. The bootstrap/origination asymmetry and the methodological character of the expression primitive receive explicit acknowledgment. The justification invokes no external authority, remains a representation rather than a higher foundation, and is fully provisional under Presumption 8.

### 1. Scope and Method of Justification

The Expressionalism system must satisfy two interlocking requirements. First, all operational parameters must be exhibited as licensed specializations of the philosophical commitments and formal apparatus, not uniquely deduced from them.

Second, every concrete element of the Toolkit must be exhibited as a direct specialization of the general hybrid function

```math
H(x) = \min\left( 1,\ \mu(x) \times P_{norm}(x\  \mid \ V) \right) \in \lbrack 0,1\rbrack
```

### 2. Foundational Principles and Constraints

The Framework establishes expression — any provisional stabilization of secondary synthesis — as the fundamental primitive, with pointing as its directed, representational, or reflexive aspect. Pointing operates in one of two primary modes: self-pointing (reflexive identity) or other-pointing (distinct repeatable references). The Non-relational Remainder remains the limit of obtainability. Isolation Residual (isolation density) sits outside the two-mode distribution and is not a third peer mode.

The formal apparatus supplies the hybrid function H(x), the dilemma gate controlled by threshold τ, and the hard isolation rule. When H(x) > τ, the analysis proceeds with relational construction; otherwise hard isolation applies.

Listed supports whose traction falls to or below τ are hard-isolated after evaluation of H(x) and the dilemma gate. They are excluded from relational aggregation, probability distributions, and yield calculations, and reported as Synthesis Refusal. Isolation Residual is the density of those refused listed supports. It is not the Non-relational Remainder. Isolation Residual never receives probability mass inside the Inferred Relator Mode Distribution and is not a third peer mode. The Non-relational Remainder remains the limit of obtainability.

The current primary expression is treated as the immediate totality and anchor (Presumption 5). Secondary expressions function as relators whose coherent meshing and pointing-back determine standing.

#### Bootstrap / Origination Asymmetry and Primitive Choice

The formal apparatus and all derived procedures are defined only for inputs already presented as primary expressions. They never claim to generate the first expression or the first secondary. First operational act, given a candidate primary $`E_{0}`$: abduct a provisional secondary set $`S_{0}`$ by applying the hybrid H under a default uniform prior and maximal σ (widest generative tension). If the resulting traction vector is entirely ≤ τ, the yield is immediately Raw/Regressed with full isolation and the chain halts (Synthesis Refusal at the system’s own conditions of possibility). Otherwise $`S_{0}`$ becomes the initial secondary layer and the ordinary sequence proceeds. Bootstrap/origination asymmetry is thereby registered as a permanent, labeled operational limit of the apparatus itself. No prior field is installed; no ex-nihilo generation is claimed. The Open Field remains the widest synthesis space available once analysis has begun.

### 3. Seven Design Criteria

Every operational parameter must satisfy the following seven jointly necessary criteria:

| Criterion | Statement | Source |
| --- | --- | --- |
| (i) Hard Isolation Preservation | Isolated components contribute no scores to any average or distribution and register only through ρ (in Tensive and in the uncertainty boost) and the reported count; post-processing character maintained | Unified Formal Model §6.2, Metrics Checks (Volume Three) §§1–4, 8, 11 |
| (ii) Two-Mode Restriction & Isolation Residual | Distribution operates exclusively over Self-pointing and Other-pointing on the non-isolated subset; Isolation Residual is defined after hard isolation as the isolation density and receives no probability mass inside the distribution | Unified Formal Model §6.3, Metrics Checks §5 |
| (iii) Primary-as-Anchor Priority | Current primary expression treated as related at ∼1 certainty; secondary expressions function as relators | Framework Presumption 5 |
