### 4. Parameter-by-Parameter Justification (Selected Key Derivations)

#### 4.1 Secondary Coherence Score Weights (0.40 × Alignment to Primary + 0.35 × Inter-Secondary Coherence + 0.25 × Traction Provided)

The primary expression functions as the anchor at ∼1 certainty (Presumption 5). Alignment to Primary therefore receives the highest weight (0.40) because it directly quantifies how coherently secondaries point back to that anchor. The values 0.40, 0.35 and 0.25 are working defaults; only their ranking has a stated reason.

These weights are meant to satisfy all seven criteria and are licensed by the anchor priority of the primary and the relational density spectrum of Presumption 3.

#### 4.1.1 Operational Definition of Inter-Secondary Coherence

The weight 0.35 assigned to Inter-Secondary Coherence is justified by the role of coherent meshing among relators as the operative engine of secondary synthesis. That meshing must itself be a direct specialization of the soft-vector apparatus already required by Alignment and by the supporting Inter-Secondary Divergence diagnostic.

After hard isolation, each non-isolated secondary i is represented by a soft distribution $`P_{i}`$ over the identical feature space used for Alignment. By default, with N here denoting the number of non-isolated survivors (not the full count used for ρ), the per-secondary Inter-Secondary Coherence is defined as:

$`{Inter\text{‑}Secondary\ Coherence}_{i} = 1`$, if $`N = 1`$

$`{Inter\text{‑}Secondary\ Coherence}_{i} = \frac{1}{N - 1}\sum_{j \neq i}\left( 1 - \operatorname{JSD}\left( P_{i} \parallel P_{j} \right) \right)`$, if N ≥ 2

This construction satisfies the seven design criteria:

- (i) Hard Isolation is respected by construction (isolated components are excluded before soft-vector formation);
- (ii)–(iii) the two-mode restriction and Isolation Residual (isolation density) remain untouched and are defined after the isolation filter;
- (iv) residual/non-relational components receive no probability mass inside the Secondary Coherence Score;
- (v) the quantity is bounded in [0,1] and numerically stable;
- (vi) the formula and soft-vector construction are fully revisable under Presumption 8 (alternative specializations such as a pre-isolation feature aggregate or a post-isolation similarity average remain available);
- (vii) it is a direct specialization of the soft-vector apparatus already required by Alignment. No new representational layer is introduced.

#### 4.2 Dilemma-Gate Threshold τ (default 0.001, adjustable [0.0001, 0.01])

The default value τ = 0.001 is a working default: low enough to keep graded sensitivity across the range of hybrid scores while still triggering hard isolation for components that have effectively lost traction. Metrics Checks §1 checked the trigger at the default and at both P8 bounds, including values just either side of each, and it matched the rule as written on the values tried. The threshold is adjustable under Presumption 8.

#### 4.3 Inferred Relator Mode Distribution

After hard isolation has been applied, the Inferred Relator Mode Distribution is a two-mode distribution over {Self-pointing, Other-pointing} that sums to 1 on the non-isolated survivors. Isolation Residual (isolation density) is defined as the proportion of components with tractionᵢ ≤ τ. It sits strictly outside the two-mode relational distribution, receives no probability mass, and indexes the density of Synthesis Refusal. When tractionᵢ ≤ τ, directedness is undefined and is labeled “pointing refused.” Isolated components are reported only as isolated gaps with the label “Synthesis Refusal active — traction ≤ τ — pointing refused (excluded from aggregation and distributions).”

The concrete feature construction that produces the two logits (or any equivalent mapping into the two-mode distribution) is not part of the core formal model. Softmax (or any equivalent normalization) remains strictly over the non-isolated subset.

The formulation is meant to satisfy all seven design criteria and constitutes a direct specialization of the relational density spectrum and the hard isolation rule.

#### 4.4 Uncertainty Boost and Clamping Bounds

**The rule:**

uncertainty_prob_global ← clamp(uncertainty_prob_global + 0.05 × Isolation Residual, 0.25, 0.9)

The bounds keep reported uncertainty from falling below 0.25 or rising above 0.9; SCS and Tensive are not bounded this way. The floor reflects that every look stays provisional and can fail in use (Presumption 8). The ceiling reflects that a run begins only once something has come forward (Presumption 1); a look in which nothing holds is handled by Hard Isolation (Unified Formal Model §6.2) and Presumption 1’s Door 2 (generative uncertainty or silence), not by the uncertainty figure, and the yield type is forced to Raw/Regressed with no score. The values 0.25 and 0.9 are working defaults, not derived; like every parameter, they are revisable under Presumption 8.

#### 4.5 Inter-Secondary Divergence (average pairwise Jensen-Shannon)

After Hard Isolation, each non-isolated secondary is treated as a soft vector. The average pairwise Jensen-Shannon divergence is computed exclusively on those soft vectors (identical support, preferred L1 or licensed alternative normalization, logarithm base 2, range [0,1]). Isolated components remain excluded by construction. Raw JSD is a supporting clash diagnostic: it never enters the Mode Distribution and is not a fourth SCS weight. 1 − JSD_avg is already the Inter-Secondary Coherence term (weight 0.35) under §4.1.1. The construction satisfies all seven design criteria:

- (i) Hard Isolation is respected by construction;
- (ii)–(iii) it never enters the Mode Distribution or SCS weights;
- (iv) residual components remain excluded;
- (v) bounded and numerically controlled;
- (vi) fully revisable under Presumption 8;
- (vii) direct specialization of the soft-vector apparatus already required by Alignment and Inter-Secondary Coherence. No new representational layer is introduced.

#### 4.6 Tsallis / Hybrid Non-Extensive Density

On the identical Phase-1 normalized membership or chunk-weight distribution already used for classical Avg Density (see the Toolkit, Phase 1: Determine Foundations), Tsallis entropy is evaluated:

$`S_{q} = \frac{1 - \sum_{i}p_{i}^{q}}{q - 1}`$, $`q \neq 1`$

```math
\phi\left( S_{q} \right) = \frac{S_{q}}{S_{q}^{\max}(k,q)}
```

```math
S_{q}^{\max}(k,q) = \frac{1 - k^{1 - q}}{q - 1}
```

where k is the support size of the Phase-1 partition. A fixed conventional value near the extensive limit (default q = 0.9) is used. The resulting ϕ lies on a scale comparable to classical Avg Density and is reported only as a supporting dual reading.

- (i) computed prior to isolation and never alters isolation decisions;
- (ii)–(iii) never enters Mode Distribution or SCS;
- (iv) residual treatment is unaffected;
- (v) bounded;
- (vi) q and the functional form are revisable under Presumption 8;
- (vii) direct specialization of the same Phase-1 distribution already required by Avg Density.

#### 4.7 Directedness differential δ (graded directed aspect of secondary synthesis relative to the primary)

After hard isolation has been applied, for each non-isolated secondary i the directedness differential $`\delta_{i}`$ ∈ [0,1] is defined as the normalized directed asymmetry between secondary i and the primary, computed on the same soft distributions as Alignment to Primary.

Preferred realization (licensed default, revisable under Presumption 8): let $`D_{i \rightarrow p}`$ = KL($`P_{i}`$ ‖ $`P_{primary}`$) and $`D_{p \rightarrow i}`$ = KL($`P_{primary}`$ ‖ $`P_{i}`$), where both distributions receive additive smoothing if necessary. Then

$`\delta_{i} = \operatorname{clamp}\left( \frac{D_{i \rightarrow p}}{D_{i \rightarrow p} + D_{p \rightarrow i} + \varepsilon},\ 0,\ 1 \right)`$, $`\varepsilon \geq 10^{- 12}`$

This construction satisfies the seven design criteria:

- (i) Hard Isolation is respected by construction and by the “pointing refused” label;
- (ii)–(iii) the two-mode restriction and Isolation Residual (isolation density) remain untouched; Isolation Residual receives no directedness;
- (iv) operational restraint toward non-relational gaps is preserved;
- (v) the quantity is bounded in [0,1] by the clamp, and ε ≥ 10⁻¹² keeps the denominator positive;
- (vi) the formula, ε, and optional weight are fully revisable under Presumption 8;
- (vii) it is a direct specialization of the soft-vector / Alignment apparatus already required by Secondary Coherence Score. No new representational layer is introduced.

### 5. Mapping from General Apparatus to Concrete Toolkit

Every concrete element of the Toolkit is exhibited as a specialization of the general hybrid function, the dilemma gate, the relational density spectrum, the hard isolation rule, and the inheritance protocol. The mapping is intended to account for every operational parameter; no external stipulation is introduced. The Toolkit therefore functions as an operational specialization of the formal apparatus, not as a higher authority or independent foundation.

The order of operations remains fixed:

hybrid evaluation (dilemma gate) → hard isolation → Isolation Residual density calculation → soft-vector construction on survivors → Secondary Coherence Score, yields, Mode Distribution, and all supporting diagnostics on survivors only.

Isolated components contribute exclusively through Isolation Residual density and the controlled uncertainty boost of §4.4. They never re-enter relational aggregation.

### 6. Governance Protocol for Parameter Evolution

Any modification of a numerical or structural parameter can itself be treated as a new primary expression subject to the full P1–P8 schema. The modification is itself open to secondary synthesis audit, Isolation Residual registration, and renewal under Presumption 8. No parameter is fixed or canonical. All coefficients, thresholds, soft-vector constructions, and optional weights remain fully revisable under the same governance.

### 7. Necessary Limits and Self-Referential Transparency

Two irreducible limits are registered transparently. First, the formal apparatus cannot analyze the conditions of its own analysis; the system cannot derive its own starting point ex nihilo. Second, the bootstrap/origination asymmetry cannot be eliminated without introducing unexamined posits that would violate Presumption 1. Total traction failure at the first operational act is a defined terminal state: the yield is immediately Raw/Regressed with full isolation and the chain halts, labeled Synthesis Refusal at the system’s own conditions of possibility (Staging Axiom). This registers the asymmetry as a permanent operational limit of the apparatus itself without installing a prior field or claiming to solve the regress. These limits are registered transparently rather than concealed. The Non-relational Remainder, the possibility of absolute chain halt at P1 Door 2 (generative uncertainty or silence), and the availability of the P8 perception gate remain untouched. The justification, like the system it describes, is provisional and subject to re-expression, replacement, or setting aside under Presumption 8.

### 8. Conclusion

All operational parameters and structural choices of the Toolkit are licensed by the philosophical commitments of the Framework and the general formal apparatus of the Unified Formal Model and are meant to satisfy the seven jointly necessary criteria. Hard isolation remains non-negotiable while active, the two-mode restriction and Isolation Residual (isolation density / density of Synthesis Refusal) are preserved, the choice of settings is declared and governed, not eliminated, and the formal-operational connection is accounted for through the mapping. The Computational Substrate thereby renders the operational system internally accounted for at the technical level without treating Isolation Residual as the Non-relational Remainder, and while keeping perpetual revisability under Presumption 8.

